# Defect 031's own reproducer, with the mark that refuses it (panel 145, landed
# 2026-09-13 as M-handle-verdict step 5).
#
# `consumes` on a C parameter says the call ENDS the life of what it is given.
# A parameter written without `@` is BORROWED: the caller keeps the value and
# reads it again after the call. Handing one to the other is a promise the
# signature cannot keep, and it is the line this file exists to refuse.
#
# **What it does not catch, and the second note on the diagnostic says so.**
# Repaired to `closed(@h: Holder)` this program compiles again, and a copy made
# BEFORE the call still holds the freed address — `--sanitize` reports
# `heap-use-after-free` on it. That class is design.md Part 8 wart 20.
#
# **The binding was `ptr consumes` until 2026-09-15.** Panel 150 R3 refuses a
# mark on a type that reaches no handle (`unread_mark`), because on a `ptr` the
# word was never read and obeying a wrong one was measured to corrupt. So the
# memory is a handle here — `record Block tag void` is C's `void *` — and the
# borrowed-handle rule below reads exactly as it did.
extern "stdlib.h"
    record Block tag void
    function malloc(size: u64) -> Block acquires free
    function free(p: Block consumes)

record Holder
    cell: Block

# The refusal. `h` is borrowed, and `free` ends what it is given.
function closed(h: Holder)
    free(h.cell)  #~ consumed_borrowed_handle

# A local is not borrowed from anybody, so consuming one is the ordinary case
# and is left alone.
function fine()
    p: Block @ malloc(size: 8)
    free(p)

# An `@` parameter says on the signature that the value does not survive, which
# is the repair the diagnostic names.
function closed_well(@h: Holder)
    free(h.cell)

function main()
    a: Holder @ Holder(cell: malloc(size: 8))
    closed(a)
    fine()
    closed_well(@a)
